WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.837
Before starting the coding exercises, make sure you can explain the main concepts in your own words.

00:00:06.837 --> 00:00:13.897
First, explain why a LEFT JOIN followed by a WHERE condition on the right table can remove unmatched

00:00:13.897 --> 00:00:14.352
left rows.

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:22.017
A strong explanation should mention that unmatched right-side values become NULL and WHERE filters the final

00:00:22.017 --> 00:00:25.287
result. Second, choose a join from a population rule.

00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:28.122
Only matched records suggests INNER JOIN.

00:00:28.122 --> 00:00:33.267
Every patient suggests preserving patients with LEFT JOIN when patients is on the left.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:37.647
Reconciliation across both sources suggests FULL OUTER JOIN.

00:00:37.647 --> 00:00:43.492
Third, explain row multiplication without simply calling the extra rows duplicates.

00:00:43.492 --> 00:00:50.372
Several child tables can contain multiple matches, creating combinations of valid detail rows.

00:00:50.372 --> 00:00:53.192
Those combinations can inflate counts.

00:00:53.192 --> 00:01:00.212
Finally, explain how to produce one row per patient safely when several child tables contain activity.

00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:06.772
One strong strategy is to summarize each child table to patient grain before joining the summaries.

00:01:06.772 --> 00:01:12.742
If you can explain these ideas clearly, the syntax becomes easier to reconstruct when you need it.
